            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "cmd.exe `/c` parser considers `&` as command separator so it ends up as:\n`start \"\" https://duckduckgo.com/?q=hello & t=brave`\n\nPossible way to fix escape `&` using `^`  in [`ui.open()`](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/master/runtime/lua/vim/ui.lua#L180-L207):\n```lua\nelseif open_cmd[1] == 'cmd.exe' and is_uri then\n\tpath = path:gsub('([&|<>^])', '^%1') -- any better way ?\nend\n```\n\nso final piece is:\n```lua\nif opt.cmd then\n  cmd = vim.list_extend(opt.cmd --[[@as string[] ]], { path })\nelse\n  local open_cmd, err = M._get_open_cmd()\n  if err then\n    return nil, err\n  end\n  ---@cast open_cmd string[]\n  if open_cmd[1] == 'xdg-open' then --could now move this block to own func?\n    job_opt.stdout = false\n    job_opt.stderr = false\n  elseif open_cmd[1] == 'cmd.exe' and is_uri then\n    path = path:gsub('([&|<>^])', '^%1')\n  end\n\n  cmd = vim.list_extend(open_cmd, { path })\nend\n```\n\nbut can't say if `path:gsub('([&|<>^])', '^%1')` is good way, but it seems [`ui.open()`](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/master/runtime/lua/vim/ui.lua#L180-L207) good place to add handling in at least",
